WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.271
"Who are you?"

00:00:02.271 --> 00:00:05.308
I would wake up in the morning,
and&nbsp;before I began my day,

00:00:05.308 --> 00:00:06.987
I would meditate on this.

00:00:06.987 --> 00:00:09.114
And with this question in mind,

00:00:09.114 --> 00:00:10.484
I kept drawing.

00:00:10.484 --> 00:00:12.153
I followed the line.

00:00:12.153 --> 00:00:13.658
I let it lead the way.

00:00:13.658 --> 00:00:16.292
And I drew myself out of a culture

00:00:16.292 --> 00:00:18.743
that was only telling&nbsp;
me what I couldn't do.

00:00:19.077 --> 00:00:21.220
The act of giving myself permission

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.722
to let go of all thoughts,

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:26.322
all fears,&nbsp;insecurities --

00:00:26.322 --> 00:00:30.568
anything that would get in the way of
 allowing myself to be completely me --

00:00:30.568 --> 00:00:33.402
that&nbsp;became my way of 
experiencing freedom.

00:00:33.402 --> 00:00:37.754
Over time, I began to create a 
bold, confident space for&nbsp;myself,

00:00:37.754 --> 00:00:39.772
a space that was all my own.

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:41.992
Initially, it was just my bedroom.

00:00:41.992 --> 00:00:45.380
But that bedroom ended&nbsp;
up in "The New York Times,"

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.259
and suddenly, I was being seen and
 known for this world I had created.

00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:53.220
Drawing has taught me how 
to create my own rules.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.569
It has taught me to open my eyes to&nbsp;
see not only what is,

00:00:57.569 --> 00:00:59.059
but what can be.

